  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 200 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe weather pattern starts to change heading into Thursday and \nFriday as a series of strong mid level shortwaves push across the \nMidwest Wednesday night and then into the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast for Thursday and Friday. Mid level troughing will extend \nwell down to the south into the Gulf and over the Southeastern \npart of the county as well as the Florida Peninsula during this \ntime frame. At the surface, an area of low pressure will dive \nacross the Midwest and into the Mid Atlantic states on Thursday \nbefore gradually pushing off the New England Coastline Thursday \nnight into Friday. The cold front associated with this \nintensifying system will extend southwestward through the \nSoutheastern States on Thursday and then across the Florida \nPeninsula Thursday night into Friday.\n\nOut ahead of this frontal boundary, the pressure gradient will \nincrease across South Florida on Thursday as winds veer to a \nsouthwesterly direction. This could create breezy and gusty \nconditions across the area during this time frame. The latest \nguidance trends seems to be leaning towards a faster frontal \npassage across the region which would occur later Thursday night \ninto early Friday morning. With guidance trending towards the\nfaster and more progressive solution, shower activity will be\nmore scattered in nature out ahead of the front from Thursday \ninto early Friday morning. This also would suggest chances for \nthunderstorm development will remain rather low as the best \ndynamics and instability associated with the system will be \nlocated well off to the northeast of the region. High temperatures\non Thursday will generally range from around 80 across the Lake \nOkeechobee region to the lower to mid 80s further to the south. \n\nBehind the front, winds will gradually shift and increase out of the \nnorthwest across the region for the rest of Friday. This will \nallow for strong cold and dry air advection to take place \nespecially heading into Friday afternoon as strong high pressure \nbegins to build in from the north. This area of high pressure will\nkeep dry conditions in place through the upcoming weekend and it \nwill usher in the coolest airmass of the fall season so far. Cold \nair advection will continue to take place through the first part \nof the weekend before winds start to shift and become more \nnortheasterly on Sunday. Before the northeasterly wind shift,  \nlow temperatures could drop into the 50s across most areas on \nFriday night into Saturday morning, and then again on Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. High temperatures on Saturday may only \nreach the upper 60s along and north of Alligator Alley due to the \nstrong cold air advection while areas south of Alligator Alley see\nhigh temperatures in the lower 70s during this time frame. High \ntemperatures on Sunday will start to moderate as the winds become \nnortheasterly and cold air advection comes to an end.",
